 {n:"The Sixth Cup",s:"A banquet table set for six and bolted to the floor. Five places have a cup, each with a dried ring of wine in the bottom. The sixth has a ring in the dust where a cup stood. The door has six small holes.",a:"The sixth cup is in this room in plain sight, doing some other job: holding a candle, propping a door. Set it in its place and pour anything into all six. The door drinks, and opens."},
 {n:"When the Sun Sleeps",s:"Two doors. Over one, a carved sun, and under it the words: I OPEN WHEN MY NAMESAKE SLEEPS. Over the other, a carved closed eye and no words. Between them stands a brazier that lights itself when anyone enters.",a:"The sun sleeps in the dark. Put out the brazier and every light the party carries, and the sun door opens. The eye door opens to any push and leads to the nearest trap, or back the way they came."},
 {n:"The Counting Stair",s:"Seven shallow steps rise to a door. Each step is carved with a number, and they are out of order: 4, 1, 6, 2, 7, 3, 5.",a:"Tread the steps in counting order, 1 to 7, which means going down as well as up. Any other order and the top step tips, sliding everyone back to the bottom. No harm. A great deal of noise."},
